📘 Time Complexity (Big-O) — Beginner Notes
Big-O ≈ how work grows when input size `n` grows.
Used in interviews to compare brute force vs smart solutions.

────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 O(1) — Constant time
Work does **not** grow with `n`.

Examples:
- Access `arr[0]`
- `x + y`
- HashMap `get` / `put` (average case)

```java
int first = arr[0]; // always ~1 step → O(1)
```

────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 O(n) — Linear time
If `n` doubles, work roughly doubles.
Usually: **one loop** over the array/string.

```java
int sum = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    sum += arr[i];
}
// n steps → O(n)
```

| n    | approx steps |
| ---- | ------------ |
| 3    | 3            |
| 3000 | 3000         |

Your examples: array sum, factorial loop, one-pass max/min.

────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 O(n²) — Quadratic time
If `n` doubles, work becomes about **4×**.
Usually: **loop inside loop** over the same size `n`.

```java
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
        // work
    }
}
// outer n × inner n = n²
```

| n    | approx steps |
| ---- | ------------ |
| 3    | 9            |
| 1000 | 1,000,000    |

────────────────────────────────────────
❓ Why nested loop ≈ O(n²)?
For **each** of `n` outer values, inner loop does about `n` work → `n × n = n²`.

Example from your code:
- `ProductOfArrayExceptSelf` brute force → O(n²)
- Smart prefix/suffix version → O(n) (two single passes)

────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 O(log n) — Logarithmic (preview)
Each step cuts problem size roughly in half.
Example: Binary search on sorted array.

n=1024 → about 10 steps (not 1024).

────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 O(n log n) — Linearithmic (preview)
Common for efficient sorting (`Arrays.sort` / merge sort style).
Better than O(n²), slower than O(n).

────────────────────────────────────────
📊 Quick compare (n = 1000)

| Complexity | Rough work |
| ---------- | ---------- |
| O(1)       | 1          |
| O(log n)   | ~10        |
| O(n)       | 1,000      |
| O(n log n) | ~10,000    |
| O(n²)      | 1,000,000  |

────────────────────────────────────────
🎤 Interview answer
“Nested loops over the same array are often O(n²) because for every outer index we scan about n inner indexes, so total operations are about n × n.”

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ How to use this while solving
1) Write brute force
2) Count loops: one loop → O(n), nested same-n loops → O(n²)
3) If n can be large (10^5) and you have O(n²) → need smarter approach
4) Optimize only after brute force is correct
